    "evidence_text": "An essential role for interferon gamma in resistance to Mycobacterium tuberculosis infection\n\nthe Development of Autoimmune Myocarditis in Mice by an\n\nTreatment with exogenous IL-12 exacerbated disease.\n\nOn the contrary, we found that IFN-gamma suppresses EAM.\n\nLack of IFN-gamma due to either depletion with an antibody or a genetic deficiency exacerbated myocarditis.\n\nTreatment of mice with recombinant IFN-gamma suppressed the development of myocarditis.   \n\n\nIn contrast, IFN-gamma plays a protective role.\n\nCD4 T cells are important in the protective immune response against tuberculosis.\n\nIn CD4 T cell-deficient mice, IFN-gamma production was due to CD8 T cells.\n\nLittle is known about its role in inflammatory heart disease.   \n\n\nMice lacking the transcription factor IRF-3 are more vulnerable to virus infection.\n\nInterferon gamma in autoimmunity: A complicated player on a complex stage.\n\nEarly views of autoimmune disease cast IFN\u03b3 as a prototypic pro-inflammatory factor.\n\nIn IFN-gammaR(-/-) mice, however, EAM is exacerbated and shows a chronic progressive disease course.\n\nIL-12p40-deficient mice on a susceptible background resisted myocarditis.",
